# Using ArchiPy Models

Data structures only — no I/O, no business rules.

Live: https://syntaxarc.github.io/ArchiPy/getting-started/project_structure/

Scaffold: `/scaffold-models` (composed by `/scaffold-domain`).

## Layout

```text
models/
├── entities/                 # SQLAlchemy BaseEntity subclasses
├── errors/                   # Domain errors extending ArchiPy BaseError hierarchy
├── types/                    # Optional enums / aliases
└── dtos/{domain}/
    ├── domain/v{n}/          # Versioned — cross service boundary
    └── repository/           # Internal — never versioned
```

## DTO Naming

| Kind | Pattern | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| Domain input | `{Op}InputDTO` | `UserRegistrationInputDTO` |
| Domain output | `{Op}OutputDTO` | `UserRegistrationOutputDTO` |
| Repository command | `{Action}CommandDTO` | `CreateUserCommandDTO` |
| Repository query | `{Action}QueryDTO` | `GetUserByIdQueryDTO` |
| Repository response | `{Domain}ResponseDTO` | `UserResponseDTO` |

DTOs should extend `archipy.models.dtos.base_dtos.BaseDTO` for ArchiPy's shared Pydantic configuration. Use plain
Pydantic `BaseModel` only when the app needs deliberately different model behavior. Prefer ArchiPy base entities where
applicable.

`BaseDTO` is frozen, strips surrounding string whitespace, validates defaults, and supports `from_attributes`. Do not
mutate DTO instances after validation.

## Pydantic Types

Use Pydantic's constrained and secret types instead of bare primitives plus `Field(...)` bounds or hand-rolled
validators:

| Need | Type |
|------|------|
| Positive / non-negative numbers | `PositiveInt`, `NonNegativeInt`, `NonNegativeFloat` |
| Tokens, passwords, API keys | `SecretStr` (call `.get_secret_value()` only at the boundary; never log it) |
| URLs, emails, IDs | `HttpUrl`, `EmailStr`, `UUID4` |
| Timestamps | `NaiveDatetime` — no timezone-aware fields; do not use `AwareDatetime` |
| Past / future constraints | `PastDatetime`, `FutureDatetime`, `PastDate`, `FutureDate` (birth dates, expiries, scheduled times) |
| String rules | `StringConstraints` / `Annotated[str, ...]` |
| Phone, country, currency, coordinates | `pydantic-extra-types` (add to the app's dependencies) |

Reference: https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/api/types/ and https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-extra-types

## Validation in DTOs

Make invalid states unrepresentable: a DTO that exists is valid. Enforce invariants at construction so logics and tests
never handle (or cover) impossible inputs — this shrinks the test state space.

- Field-level rules not expressible as a type: `@field_validator("x")` (normalise, cross-check one field).
- Cross-field rules: `@model_validator(mode="after")` (e.g. `start < end`, "exactly one of `a` / `b`", `max >= min`,
  field required only when another is set).
- Prefer a type (see Pydantic Types) over a validator; prefer a validator over a check in a logic or service.
- Model mutually exclusive shapes as a discriminated union (`Annotated[A | B, Field(discriminator="kind")]`) or
  `Literal` / `StrEnum` fields instead of optional fields plus runtime `if`s; avoid boolean flags that gate other fields.
- Raise `ValueError` with a clear message inside validators (Pydantic wraps it in `ValidationError`); the
  transport layer surfaces it as an invalid-argument error — do not re-validate downstream.
- Validators are pure: no I/O, no DB lookups, no clocks beyond Pydantic's date types. Uniqueness / existence checks
  belong in logics.
- Test each validator once at the DTO boundary (one invalid example per rule); logics and BDD scenarios then cover only
  valid states, not the cross-product of invalid combinations.

## gRPC DTOs (`BaseProtobufDTO`)

For gRPC domain DTOs extend `archipy.models.dtos.base_protobuf_dto.BaseProtobufDTO` (a `BaseDTO`, so every validator
above applies), set `_proto_class` to the generated message, and convert with `from_proto(request)` / `to_proto()`.
Needs the `archipy[grpc]` / protobuf extra.

- `from_proto` is the validation boundary: it runs `model_validate`, so field/model validators reject bad requests
  before the logic. Do not re-check in the servicer.
- It uses `always_print_fields_with_no_presence=True`: unset proto3 scalars arrive as `""` / `0` / `False`, not
  missing. Constrain them (`PositiveInt`, `StringConstraints(min_length=1)`) so "unset" is rejected, and mark
  `optional` proto fields `T | None` when absence is meaningful.
- Pydantic `ValidationError` is not an ArchiPy error: catch it in the servicer (or interceptor) and raise
  `InvalidArgumentError ... from e` so gRPC status maps to `INVALID_ARGUMENT`.
- `to_proto` dumps `model_dump(mode="json")`: `SecretStr` becomes a masked string and non-JSON types change shape.
  Keep secrets / validator-only fields out of response DTOs, and use `from_proto` for requests, `to_proto` for
  responses only.
- Field names must match the `.proto` names (`preserving_proto_field_name=True`); unknown request keys are ignored,
  unknown DTO fields make `ParseDict` fail.
- Keep proto types out of logics: convert in the servicer, pass the DTO inward.

## Errors

- Subclass ArchiPy errors from `archipy.models.errors` (e.g. `AlreadyExistsError`, `NotFoundError`).
- Prefer an existing exported error over creating a near-duplicate.
- Custom `BaseError` subclasses define stable `code`, localized `message_en` / `message_fa`, and transport
  `http_status` / `grpc_status` class attributes.
- Pass context through constructor arguments / `additional_data`; do not mutate error codes or messages per instance.
- Raise with context: `raise NotFoundError(...) from e`.
- Never raise bare `Exception` for domain failures.
- Group app-specific errors by concern under `models/errors/` (for example `validation_errors.py`,
  `resource_errors.py`, and `business_errors.py`) and subclass the closest ArchiPy error.
- Export public app errors from `models/errors/__init__.py` when other layers consume them.
- With `AppUtils.create_fastapi_app` exception handlers, ArchiPy errors map to HTTP status automatically — see https://syntaxarc.github.io/ArchiPy/tutorials/error_handling/

## Do Not

- Put adapters, DB sessions, or HTTP/gRPC types in models.
- Version repository DTOs.
- Import repositories, logics, or services from models.
